\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Shows explicitly that identical linear instability data permit arbitrary nonlinear saturated amplitudes in a mode model.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{An amplitude-identifiability test.} Suppose a particular linearly unstable eddy mode has complex amplitude $A$ and growth rate $\sigma>0$. Every nonlinear model in the family
\[
 \dot A=\sigma A-g|A|^2A,\qquad g>0,
\]
has the identical linearization $\dot A=\sigma A$ at zero. Writing $R=|A|^2$ gives $\dot R=2\sigma R-2gR^2$, whose solution for $R(0)=R_0>0$ is
\[
 R(t)=\frac{\sigma/g}{1+(\sigma/(gR_0)-1)e^{-2\sigma t}}.
\]
Direct substitution verifies the expression, and $R(t)\to\sigma/g$. Varying $g$ leaves all linear mode shapes and growth rates unchanged while changing the saturated amplitude arbitrarily.

\paragraph{What linear predictions can still test.} At early times with $gR_0e^{2\sigma t}\ll\sigma$, the solution is close to $R_0e^{2\sigma t}$. The regime ends on a time scale comparable to $(2\sigma)^{-1}\log(\sigma/(gR_0))$ when the logarithm is positive. Thus an observed scale or initial growth can be compared with a linear instability, but mature amplitude is not determined by the linearization alone. At $R_0=0$ the solution stays zero, another reminder that excitation must be specified.

\paragraph{Source-scope gap.} This is an exactly solved mode model, not a fitted ocean model or a counterexample to the report's broad null hypothesis. Whether observed eddy shapes and scales are ``largely'' predicted requires a chosen mean state, data, error criterion and nonlinear saturation information. None is supplied here. The first obstacle to an amplitude prediction is identifying the analogue of $g$ or proving it is irrelevant to the chosen observable.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked logistic solution, zero initial amplitude, early-time regime and separation of scale/growth predictions from amplitude predictions. Model self-audit only; no independent human proof checking. Prompts A and B were applied to this canonical record and its qualified source scope. Checked logistic solution, zero initial amplitude, early-time regime and separation of scale/growth predictions from amplitude predictions. Retained unresolved status and zero original-target progress; A specified ocean baseline/data metric and control of nonlinear saturation for the selected observables are absent.

Coordinator checked the logistic equation for squared amplitude, its positive limiting value and the dependence on the undetermined nonlinear coefficient. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

\paragraph{Tools, process and contributions.}
Python 3 was used for file preparation and structural checks; no numerical experiment or theorem prover was used. Contribution prompt A was followed by prompt B. GPT-6 Astra with ultra reasoning drafted the research and performed its stated self-audit; any additional coordinator contribution is identified in the verification and audit. A GPT-6-based Codex coordinator assembled the manuscript and checked repository integration; its exact variant and reasoning configuration were not exposed. No human mathematical contribution, independent human proof check or formal proof certification is supplied. Complete derivations and the consulted source references are included here; private reasoning transcripts are not published.

\paragraph{Final status and first remaining gap.}
